{
 "id": "E05",
 "title": "The Port Where Boxes Change Ships",
 "seriesTitle": "INDIA MEGAPROJECTS",
 "world": "strata",
 "fps": 30,
 "seed": "s03e05",
 "passages": [
  {
   "id": "P01",
   "text": "Most ports are places where goods arrive to be used. This one is a place where goods change ships. A giant vessel arrives, lifts off the boxes meant for other ports, and sails on. Smaller ships carry those boxes the last part of the journey.",
   "estSec": 18.6,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 45
  },
  {
   "id": "P02",
   "text": "Vizhinjam International Seaport, in Kerala, was inaugurated on the second of May 2025. The Press Information Bureau calls it India's first dedicated container transshipment port, built at about eight thousand eight hundred crore rupees.",
   "estSec": 14,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 34
  },
  {
   "id": "P03",
   "text": "There is a reason to care that is not about size. The Press Information Bureau says the port reduces India's reliance on foreign ports, and the Union Minister of Ports said its expansion will help cut India's dependence on foreign transshipment hubs. A port decides where a country's boxes change ships, and who earns from the change.",
   "estSec": 23.6,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 57
  },
  {
   "id": "P04",
   "text": "Idea one: a hub. Think of an airline hub. You do not fly from every small town to every other small town. You fly to a hub, then out again. Boxes do the same. Before a hub, every route needs its own big ship. After it, one big ship feeds many small ones.",
   "estSec": 21.9,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 53
  },
  {
   "id": "P05",
   "text": "Idea two: depth. A loaded giant ship sits deep in the water, the way a loaded truck needs a strong road. It needs deep water at the berth. This port sits close to a major shipping lane, with deep water near the shore, so less digging is needed. The Press Information Bureau says it can host the world's largest cargo ships.",
   "estSec": 25.2,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 61
  },
  {
   "id": "P06",
   "text": "Idea three: speed. Time in port is money for every box on board. Think of a racing pit stop, where every second is practised. The Press Information Bureau describes Vizhinjam as India's first automated port, with large-scale automation for quick turnaround. Before the machines, a ship waits. After them, it leaves.",
   "estSec": 21.1,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 51
  },
  {
   "id": "P07",
   "text": "Now, what India built, and what is still unfinished. Phase one began commercial operations on the third of December 2024, with a designed capacity of one million containers a year. Since then, it has handled more than one point four three million, running at over one hundred and thirty percent of its design. That is a sign of demand, and also a limit. Work on phases two to four has begun, with completion targeted for December 2028.",
   "estSec": 31.8,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 77
  },
  {
   "id": "P08",
   "text": "A port is not ranked by size alone. A transshipment hub, a gateway for local goods and a fishing harbour solve different problems. Vizhinjam is a hub. It does not replace every other port.",
   "estSec": 14,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 34
  },
  {
   "id": "P09",
   "text": "A fictional morning: a giant ship is waiting offshore, and its berth is busy. Every hour of waiting is a cost for every box on board. A port that is full is a port that is wanted, and also a port that is late.",
   "estSec": 18.2,
   "flag": "FICTIONAL SCENARIO",
   "words": 44
  },
  {
   "id": "P10",
   "text": "One idea about people: demand grows to fill new capacity. Economists studying American cities found that when road capacity grows, driving grows with it. This port is already running above its designed capacity. Space built for today fills up tomorrow.",
   "estSec": 16.5,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 40
  },
  {
   "id": "P11",
   "text": "Three things to remember. A hub lets one big ship feed many small ones. Deep water near the shore is the first asset. And a full port is a signal to expand. India built the hub. Now the question is twenty twenty-eight: will the capacity keep up with the boxes?",
   "estSec": 20.7,
   "flag": "",
   "words": 50
  }
 ],
